{"meta": {"chapter_title": "Educational Technology Evaluation", "title": "Evidence Claims and Vendor Claims", "description": "CAT 531 textbook section connecting evidence claims and vendor claims to Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "course": "cat531-supplement", "chapter": "05", "section": "02", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."], "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation", "cat531_supplement_evidence_claims_and_vendor_claims", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "prereq_section_ids": ["cat531-supplement/05/01"], "practice_ids": ["cat531_supplement_5_2_evidence_claims_and_vendor_claims_artifact_signal", "cat531_supplement_5_2_evidence_claims_and_vendor_claims_annotation_signal", "cat531_supplement_5_2_evidence_claims_and_vendor_claims_ai_boundary", "cat531_supplement_5_2_evidence_claims_and_vendor_claims_calibration"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 2, "top_tier_hardened": false}, "content": "# Evidence Claims and Vendor Claims\n\n![Evidence Claims and Vendor Claims textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/05-02-evidence-claims-and-vendor-claims.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA vendor's job is to make a tool sound effective; a teacher's job is to find out whether it is. Those are not the same task, and the gap between them is where most weak adoption decisions live. \"Boosts scores 35 percent,\" \"research-backed,\" \"trusted by 10,000 classrooms\": each phrase is engineered to read as evidence while often resting on a study the vendor funded, a sample of a dozen students, or no comparison group at all. This section teaches teachers to slow down at exactly the moment a claim sounds strongest and ask what kind of evidence, if any, stands behind it.\n\nThe skill is not cynicism. Some vendor claims are well supported, and dismissing every claim is as lazy as believing every claim. The professional move is to grade each claim by the evidence that backs it, then decide how much weight that evidence can bear. A teacher who can do this protects students from tools that work only in a brochure.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Separate a vendor's claim from the evidence offered for it, naming the study design behind any effect size.\n- Identify the three weaknesses that most often inflate edtech claims: no control group, tiny or self-selected samples, and a conflicted funder.\n- Assign an evidence weight to a claim and justify the weight from the design, not the headline number.\n- Document a decision that keeps a promising tool on the table while logging its claim as unproven.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA vendor pitches a writing-feedback tool with the headline \"Boosts writing scores by 35 percent, peer-reviewed.\" The number is large, the word peer-reviewed is reassuring, and the temptation is to write \"adopt.\" But the cited study turns out to have been run by the vendor's own team, on 11 students, over three weeks, with no comparison class. The 35 percent is simply the rise from a pretest to a posttest.\n\nThe decision worth recording is not whether the tool is good or bad. It is whether the teacher can name precisely why a 35 percent pre-post gain with no control and n = 11 cannot, on its own, justify a purchase, and what evidence would.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA **claim** is an assertion of effect (\"improves writing\"). **Evidence** is the design and data offered to support it. The two are routinely conflated because vendors present evidence-shaped language (\"35 percent,\" \"study\") in place of evidence-grade design. Reading critically means refusing to let a number stand in for the design that produced it.\n\nThree weaknesses inflate edtech claims more than any others, and each has a plain-language test:\n\n- **No control group.** A pre-post gain cannot separate the tool from everything else that happened (normal teaching, maturation, practice effects). Ask: compared to what? If there is no comparison, the gain is uninterpretable as a tool effect.\n- **Small or self-selected sample.** Eleven students, or volunteers who opted in, cannot represent a class of 24, let alone a district. Ask: who, and how many, and were they chosen or did they choose?\n- **Conflicted funder.** A study run or paid for by the seller has an incentive baked in. Ask: who would lose money if this study had found no effect?\n\nEach weakness lowers the **evidence weight** you assign, regardless of how large the headline effect is. A 35 percent gain on a flawed design carries less weight than a 6 percent gain from a randomized comparison.\n\nThe explorer below makes that arithmetic visible. Evidence weight is built from the design (a control multiplier, a sample factor, an independence factor), not from the headline number. Push the effect size as high as you like; if the design factors stay low, the trustworthy weight stays low. The model multiplies a base credibility by each design factor and then scales the raw effect down to what the design can actually support.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Evidence Weight Comes From Design, Not the Headline Number\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"effect\",\"label\":\"Headline effect size claimed\",\"min\":0,\"max\":50,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"%\",\"default\":35},{\"name\":\"control\",\"label\":\"Comparison-group factor (0 = none, 1 = randomized control)\",\"min\":0,\"max\":1,\"step\":0.1,\"unit\":\"x\",\"default\":0},{\"name\":\"sample\",\"label\":\"Sample adequacy factor (0 = n~11, 1 = n~400)\",\"min\":0,\"max\":1,\"step\":0.1,\"unit\":\"x\",\"default\":0.1},{\"name\":\"indep\",\"label\":\"Funder independence factor (0 = vendor-run, 1 = neutral)\",\"min\":0,\"max\":1,\"step\":0.1,\"unit\":\"x\",\"default\":0}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Design credibility (0 to 1)\",\"unit\":\"\",\"expr\":\"control * sample * indep\"},{\"label\":\"Trustworthy effect the design can support\",\"unit\":\"%\",\"expr\":\"effect * control * sample * indep\"},{\"label\":\"Inflation gap (headline minus trustworthy)\",\"unit\":\"%\",\"expr\":\"effect - effect * control * sample * indep\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"control\",\"output\":1}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\n<inline-check question=\"The vendor's claim is 35 percent with no control, n=11, and vendor funding. A district reports 6 percent from a randomized study of 400 students with neutral funding. Which claim should carry more weight in your matrix?\" explanation=\"The 6 percent claim. Evidence weight tracks design, not effect size. The randomized, large, independent study can actually support its number as a tool effect, while the 35 percent rests on a pre-post gain with no comparison, a tiny sample, and a conflicted funder. The larger number is the less trustworthy one.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The 35 percent, because the effect is much larger.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The 6 percent, because its design (control, large sample, neutral funder) can support the number as a tool effect.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Neither; both are too small to matter.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nThree course anchors sharpen the read. **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)** asks whether the claimed outcome is even the learning you care about, since a tool can raise a narrow score while teaching little. **Critical edtech studies** names the marketing machinery and the incentive to overstate. **[AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)** flags claims about \"personalization\" or \"AI accuracy\" that hide what data the tool collects to make them.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nA first draft often hides the real decision behind a vague improvement claim:\n\n> I made the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision clearer.\n\nThat claim is too thin because \"clearer\" does not identify the audience, the problem, or the evidence. A stronger claim would read:\n\n> I revised the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision so that the intended audience can see the decision, the constraint, and the evidence source. The revision is justified by **critical edtech studies**, and I can point to the exact part of the artifact that changed.\n\nNotice the shift: the artifact is no longer just polished. The revision names the evidence source and preserves the reason a suggestion was accepted or rejected.\n\n### Worked Example: Separating a Vendor Claim From Evidence\n\nScenario: a vendor pitches a writing-feedback tool with the headline \"Boosts writing scores by 35 percent.\"\n\n1. **Found the claim as written (before).** The brochure says \"35 percent improvement, peer-reviewed study.\" A teacher annotates: \"Sounds strong, adopt.\" No source examined yet.\n2. **Traced the claim to its study.** The cited study was run by the vendor's own team on 11 students for 3 weeks, with no comparison group. The \"35 percent\" was the rise from a pretest to a posttest with no control.\n3. **Reweighed each claim.** Vendor claim: 35 percent gain. Evidence weight: low, because there is no control group, the sample is 11, and the funder is the vendor. A pre-post rise with no comparison cannot separate the tool from normal instruction.\n4. **Annotated judgment call (after).** The revised note reads: \"Claim kept on the table but logged as low-weight, conflicted, n=11, no control. Decision: pilot in one section ourselves with a comparison class before any purchase. We rejected the brochure's framing of 35 percent as established evidence.\"\n\nThe brief is now defensible because a reviewer sees the exact gap between what the vendor claimed and what the evidence can actually support.\n\nTake the 35 percent claim apart one design fact at a time. At each step, predict what the next fact does to the claim's credibility before you reveal it.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Disassembling the 35 percent writing-feedback claim\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The brochure says \\\"Boosts writing scores by 35 percent, peer-reviewed study.\\\" Before checking anything, what is the right first move?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Write \\\"adopt, 35 percent\\\" in the matrix\",\"Record the claim verbatim and resist scoring it\",\"Halve the number to be safe\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Record the claim verbatim and resist scoring it. \\\"Peer-reviewed\\\" names a process, not a verdict; a weak study can survive review. The number stays unscored until you find the design behind it.\"},{\"prompt\":\"You locate the study. It compared a pretest to a posttest with no separate comparison class. What can the 35 percent NOT do?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"It cannot separate the tool from normal teaching, maturation, and practice\",\"It cannot be expressed as a percentage\",\"It cannot exceed 100 percent\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"With no control group, the 35 percent cannot be read as a tool effect. The gain could come from ordinary instruction over three weeks. Ask: compared to what? There is no answer here.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The sample is 11 students, recruited by the vendor team. What does that do to generalization?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Nothing; 11 is plenty\",\"It cannot represent a class of 24, let alone a district, and may be self-selected\",\"It makes the effect larger\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Eleven students, possibly volunteers, cannot stand in for your roster. Ask: who, how many, and chosen or self-selecting? Each answer here lowers the weight further.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Final design facts: the study was run and funded by the seller, who profits if it shows an effect. Verdict: keep the tool on the table but log the claim at low weight (no control, n=11, vendor-funded). Next step is a controlled local pilot you run, not a purchase justified by the vendor slide. Claim to design to weight to bounded next step is the whole discipline.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nUse the claimed outcome first, the study design second, and the funder third. A claim earns weight only when the design can actually support the size of the effect it asserts.\n\nWhen a vendor number looks persuasive, run it through three tests before it enters the matrix:\n\n1. **Design check:** Was there a comparison group? A pre-post-only result cannot be read as a tool effect.\n2. **Sample check:** How many students, and were they chosen or self-selected? Small or volunteer samples cannot generalize to your class.\n3. **Funder check:** Who paid for or ran the study, and who profits if it shows an effect?\n\nA claim that fails any of these is not banned from your evaluation; it is simply logged at low weight, with a note on what evidence (a controlled pilot, an independent study) would raise it.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nOne misreading is to treat \"peer-reviewed\" or \"research-backed\" as a verdict rather than a starting point. Those phrases describe a process, not a result, and a weak study can survive review. The opposite misreading is to dismiss every vendor claim as marketing; that throws away tools that may genuinely help and replaces evidence with reflex.\n\nA defensible brief shows the claim, the design behind it, and the weight assigned, so a colleague can see that the conclusion tracks the evidence rather than the headline.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nTake the 35 percent claim and walk it to the floor. First, write the claim verbatim and resist scoring it. Then locate the study it rests on and extract three facts: comparison group (none), sample (11), funder (the vendor). Each fact is a deduction from the claim's credibility, and you record the deduction rather than the impression.\n\nNext, ask the [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge) question the brochure skips: 35 percent of what? If the posttest rewarded surface features the tool happens to coach, the number could rise while real writing did not improve. The claimed outcome and the valued outcome may not be the same thing, and the brief must say so.\n\nFinally, convert the read into a decision that wastes neither the tool nor the truth. The tool stays a candidate, but its claim is logged as low-weight and conflicted, and the next step is a controlled pilot you run, not a purchase you justify with the vendor's slide. That sequence, claim to design to weight to bounded next step, is the whole discipline.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: **claim** on the left, **evidence weight and why** on the right. For each vendor assertion, write the design fact that raises or lowers its weight. The column on the right should never just repeat the headline number; it should explain what the design can and cannot support.\n\nA strong reader of this section can answer four questions:\n\n1. What exactly is being claimed, and what outcome does it really measure?\n2. What study design stands behind the claim, and what are its weaknesses?\n3. What weight does that design justify, independent of the effect size?\n4. What evidence would raise the weight enough to justify adoption?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nFor this section, the brief is strong when every claim is paired with its evidence weight and the reason for that weight, so a mentor can audit whether the conclusion follows the evidence. Treat a large headline number as a prompt to investigate, never as a column total. If the only support is a vendor-run pre-post study, the brief should record it as such and route to a local pilot.\n\nA useful closing line would sound like this: \"I changed the brief so the 35 percent claim is logged at low weight with its design noted (no control, n=11, vendor-funded). I kept out the suggestion to feature the 35 percent in the recommendation because the design cannot support a number that large as a tool effect.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove from a vendor claim to an independent claim: a district study, run by a neutral research office, reports a 6 percent gain from a randomized comparison across 400 students. The same three tests now run in the tool's favor: there is a control, the sample is large, the funder is neutral. The smaller number deserves more weight than the vendor's larger one. Use this reversal to confirm that you are grading evidence by design, not by the size of the number or the warmth of the source.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/05-02-evidence-claims-and-vendor-claims-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"05.02\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 05.02. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation\",\"cat531_supplement_evidence_claims_and_vendor_claims\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_evidence_claims_and_vendor_claims\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your brief records each claim verbatim, the study design behind it, the evidence weight you assigned and why, and the bounded next step (often a local pilot) for any claim too weak to justify purchase on its own.\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat531_supplement_5_2_evidence_claims_and_vendor_claims_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_evidence_claims_and_vendor_claims", "stem": "A vendor brochure states: 'Students who use our app improve reading by 40 percent.' What is missing before a teacher can treat this as evidence?", "options": ["A more colorful chart of the 40 percent figure.", "The comparison group, the measure, the sample, and who funded the study.", "A testimonial from another school.", "Confirmation that 40 percent sounds large."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A percentage with no comparison group, outcome measure, sample, or funding disclosure is a marketing claim, not evidence. Those details determine whether the number means anything.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_5_2_number_equals_evidence", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation", "cat531_supplement_evidence_claims_and_vendor_claims", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_5_2_evidence_claims_and_vendor_claims_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_evidence_claims_and_vendor_claims", "stem": "A vendor cites 'a peer-reviewed study' showing gains. The study was conducted by the company's own research division on a 9-student pilot. How should a teacher weight this?", "options": ["As strong independent evidence because it is peer reviewed.", "As a low-weight, conflicted source; small internal pilots need independent replication before they carry decision weight.", "As irrelevant because all vendor studies are worthless.", "As decisive because the company has expertise in the product."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Peer review does not erase a conflict of interest or a tiny sample. The claim can stay on the table but carries little weight until an independent study with adequate sample replicates it.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_5_2_peer_reviewed_removes_conflict", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_educational_technology_evaluation", "cat531_supplement_evidence_claims_and_vendor_claims"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_5_2_evidence_claims_and_vendor_claims_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning", "stem": "You paste a vendor's claims into an AI tool and ask it to 'check if these are true.' The AI returns a confident summary saying the claims are well supported. What is the defensible next step?", "options": ["Record the AI summary as verification and adopt the tool.", "Treat the AI summary as a lead, then trace each claim to a primary source you can read and weigh yourself.", "Ask the AI to rate the vendor out of 10.", "Trust the AI because it has read more studies than any teacher."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "An AI cannot confirm a vendor claim it has not independently sourced, and it may echo the vendor's framing. Use it to locate sources, then verify the primary evidence yourself.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_5_2_ai_confirms_vendor_claims", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ai_supported_learning", "cat531_supplement_evidence_claims_and_vendor_claims"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_5_2_evidence_claims_and_vendor_claims_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision", "stem": "A vendor reports that 'engagement rose 3x.' A teacher wants to know whether this predicts learning. What distinction must the teacher hold?", "options": ["Engagement and learning are the same outcome.", "Engagement is a proxy; a rise in time-on-task does not by itself show that targeted learning improved.", "Three times is always meaningful regardless of measure.", "Engagement metrics are the only reliable class